WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:03.545
Wesley, as challenging as some
of our days might be with him,

00:00:03.545 --> 00:00:04.963
I wouldn't want to change
him for the world

00:00:04.963 --> 00:00:07.215
because he is just the
happiest little thing.

00:00:08.049 --> 00:00:10.051
My name is Jim Baird

00:00:10.051 --> 00:00:11.803
and I am Tori Baird.

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:14.806
We have two boys, Wesley and Hudson.

00:00:15.098 --> 00:00:18.059
Wesley is just a little light for us.

00:00:18.226 --> 00:00:22.522
Wesley's non-verbal, but yet he
finds a way to say so much,

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:25.942
just with his expressions
and his million-dollar smile.

00:00:27.444 --> 00:00:31.156
We noticed around four months,
Wesley wasn't tracking with his eyes.

00:00:31.156 --> 00:00:34.409
He's not reaching for toys
the way he typically should be.

00:00:34.451 --> 00:00:38.913
He's still not walking or crawling
or making really good eye contact.

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:42.250
So we're kind of coming
to our own conclusion

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.711
that obviously something's going on here.

00:00:45.795 --> 00:00:49.507
We just felt like all this kind of joy
and the idea of how things

00:00:49.507 --> 00:00:53.511
were gonna be as new parents and
all the hopes and dreams we saw for

00:00:53.511 --> 00:00:57.307
our kid were replaced with
concern and worry and fear.

00:00:57.599 --> 00:01:00.727
Our genetics counselor, I remember
her saying, the bloodwork came back

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:05.482
and Wesley has a syndrome called FoxG1,
and she's like, “How do you feel?”

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:09.527
And I remember thinking,
I don't know what FoxG1 is.

00:01:09.527 --> 00:01:11.279
I have no idea what you're saying to me.

00:01:11.279 --> 00:01:14.115
And she kind of went through her
paperwork and explained it to us.

00:01:14.949 --> 00:01:19.621
FoxG1 Syndrome is a monogenic
neurodevelopmental disorder.

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:23.500
It is caused by the mutation
of the FoxG1 gene.

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:26.836
FoxG1 gene is actually one of
the first and most

00:01:26.836 --> 00:01:29.923
critical genes in early brain development.

00:01:30.507 --> 00:01:34.302
I could feel my heart being ripped
out of my chest at this point,

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:38.431
and I just remember sitting there
with Wesley on my lap and holding him

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:39.557
tighter and tighter.

00:01:41.351 --> 00:01:42.227
They give you this

00:01:42.227 --> 00:01:45.939
life-changing news that they don't
have very much information about.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:47.232
They don't know what it could mean

00:01:47.232 --> 00:01:50.860
long-term for Wesley,
what his life is gonna look like.

00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:53.863
Jim was driving and we had to
pull over at one point and

00:01:53.863 --> 00:01:58.034
just, like, cry and we're googling FoxG1.

00:01:58.034 --> 00:02:01.371
And I remember
reading what the symptoms are.

00:02:01.412 --> 00:02:03.832
One of them was uncontrollable laughter.

00:02:03.915 --> 00:02:08.253
Wesley started laughing in the back seat,
while Jim and I are, like,

00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:09.796
horribly devastated.

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:12.549
And I remember thinking,
maybe it's gonna be okay.

00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:17.053
Maybe this isn't so bad if one of
his symptoms is laughing, you know?

00:02:17.929 --> 00:02:21.307
Me and Tori just stood up
straight and looked at each other

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:25.061
and just basically said that it was
gonna be business as usual.

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:26.604
I just looked at him

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:29.482
and I told him that he was going to be
my best bud no matter what.

00:02:30.066 --> 00:02:34.612
It's not a super huge community of people
that have been diagnosed with FoxG1

00:02:34.612 --> 00:02:39.367
because it is still fairly
new as far as syndromes go,

00:02:39.367 --> 00:02:43.329
but there's likely more people out there
who have not been diagnosed.

00:02:44.455 --> 00:02:47.709
We have a little Facebook group
of caregivers of those

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:51.045
with FoxG1, so we reach out to each other
and ask, “Do you deal with this?

00:02:51.045 --> 00:02:51.838
How do you deal with that?”

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:55.008
And it's also just nice to know
that there are other people out there

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:58.511
who know what we're going through,
and it gives us hope.

00:02:59.137 --> 00:03:02.891
One of the things that makes Wesley
the happiest is when he hears

00:03:02.891 --> 00:03:07.187
all the birds chirping,
and he just has a big smile on his face.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.481
We're lucky enough to have those outdoor
skills and knowledge.

00:03:10.815 --> 00:03:15.403
Being in nature is good for anyone,
but it's especially good for Wesley.

00:03:16.738 --> 00:03:19.991
Wesley became more aware
of his surroundings,

00:03:19.991 --> 00:03:22.202
and that came with being outdoors.

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:24.996
There's no cure for FoxG1 Syndrome.

00:03:25.163 --> 00:03:28.541
You could sink into a really sad state.

00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:30.293
When you get into that state,

00:03:30.293 --> 00:03:34.839
you're unable to find the energy to care
for your child who has disabilities.

00:03:34.839 --> 00:03:36.591
You're unable to take care of yourself.

00:03:36.591 --> 00:03:39.302
It's gonna have its challenges,
but at the end of the day,

00:03:39.302 --> 00:03:43.556
you're gonna love them anyways,
and they're gonna teach you so much.

00:03:43.598 --> 00:03:46.392
Every time you fall down into
these negative thoughts,

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:48.478
you have to replace them
with positive thoughts.

00:03:48.853 --> 00:03:51.940
I have confidence that
Wesley is gonna progress

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.777
and there might be more treatments
that could help Wesley, that his brother’s

00:03:55.777 --> 00:03:58.821
gonna be there to help him,
and essentially they're gonna lead

00:03:58.821 --> 00:04:03.701
fulfilled lives and do good for the world.

